From 4740d09e98d0a18cc157aa151301d16f4cdc8ea1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TC Lowe <111537227+TCLowe1982@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:31:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(generation): generic prompt-context contributions
 for capability packages
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Capability packages can supply retrieved context to a generation, but only
through a feature-specific Engine module: long-term-memory-runtime.ts
resolves one hard-coded service key. Every further contributor needs its
own Engine file and its own call site, so the integration cost falls on
the Engine rather than on the package.

This states the contract once. A package registers a service under
`<agentId>:prompt-context`, where the agent id is one it already declares.
The generation route knows which agents are active for a chat, so the key
is derived from data the caller holds — no registry change, no discovery
mechanism, and no third-party name in Engine source.

Behaviour matches the existing long-term-memory path: calls are bounded by
AGENT_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS, contributions are trimmed and capped at 100k
characters, empty results are dropped, and failures are logged and skipped
so a misbehaving package degrades to "no extra context" rather than a
failed turn. Contributors run in the caller's agent order so injection is
deterministic across turns. Acceptance reporting is optional and is kept
separate from collection, because assembling a contribution and shipping
it are different events.

Additive only. getCapabilityService returns null for unregistered keys, so
an install with no participating packages does one map lookup per active
agent and contributes nothing. The long-term-memory path is untouched.

Adds scripts/regressions/prompt-context-contributors.regression.ts,
covering the no-package no-op, ordering, trimming, the 100k cap, empty and
null contributions, abort handling, timeout, and containment of both
contribution and accounting failures. Wired into `pnpm regression`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
